  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 311 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A ridge of high pressure is centered near the \nInternational Border early this morning, providing us with ideal\nconditions for frost in the Adirondack region and far \nnortheastern Vermont. The center of high pressure is expected to\nbecome positioned over the Gulf of Maine by this afternoon, \nleading to southerly channeled flow. Given dry and relatively \ncool air in place, Lake Champlain should once again have rather \nbreezy conditions beginning this afternoon with a long duration \nof south winds through tomorrow morning.\n\nAside from the local terrain effects and modest low level jet \ntonight, no significant weather is expected as a weakening cold \nfront approaches from the west tonight. Other than increasing \nclouds and milder conditions, there will be spotty light showers\npossible, with a broad consensus on the timing being in the \nearly Wednesday morning hours and slightly greater chance of \nmeasurable rain in southern portions of our northern New York \nzones. Because the front largely washes out over/just south of \nour region, a large area of cloudy skies will probably be \npresent during the day, which will possibly limit heating on \nwhat otherwise would be the warmest day of the week given fairly\nwarm temperatures aloft. 925 millibar temperatures especially \nover our southern areas will likely be above normal (near the \n75th percentile) during the afternoon, supporting highs near 80 \nin the Upper Valley especially with mainly sunny skies or breaks\nin the clouds. With dewpoints increasing back into the low 60s,\nit will be another short-lived bout of summer weather.\n\nThe next more substantial rainfall chance remains on track for \nThursday associated with weak overrunning, as high pressure \nremains to our north and a wave of low pressure passes to our \nsouth. The thermal gradient and dynamical support for rain looks\nmodest at best, so the footprint and intensity of rain should \nlikewise be limited. Trends in the data show better chances of \nshowers generally in the afternoon/evening and across \nsouthern/central Vermont and southern portions of the \nAdirondacks. Rain cooled air will complicate the temperature \nforecast especially with an earlier onset of precipitation, but \nfor many locations Thursday looks like the last day for a while \nto see temperatures reach the 70s.\n\nA trough passing through Quebec will force a cold front through\nour region Thursday night with little fanfare yet again, but \nbring in another round of refreshing air on northwest flow. \nNothing particularly noteworthy with this air mass appears in \nthe data at this time with only modest advection; even in the \nglobal ensemble cluster with the lowest temperatures they only \ndrop a bit below seasonal normals. As such, high temperatures \nranging from the mid 60s to low 70s amidst full sunshine look \nreasonable at this time. The most cool and dry air appears to be\ntimed for Friday night as high pressure builds in from the \nnorthwest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Focus for the longer term portion of the \nforecast continues to be the low pressure system bringing rain \nto our region Saturday night into Sunday. Surface low will track\nfrom the Great Lakes region northeastward just north of our \narea. There are still some differences between models with this \nsystem, but general thought is for rain to spread into our \nregion Saturday night, continuing into Sunday with most areas \nhaving a wetting rain. ECMWF does keep this system mostly south \nof our area, but GFS solution shows most areas having rain. WPC \nhas placed our region in Marginal risk for the excessive rain \noutlook.",
